       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 30 mg of amlodipine once daily is three times the FDA-labeled maximum of 10 mg per day. At that dose, the risks include severe hypotension, reflex tachycardia, peripheral edema, and, in overdose, prolonged vasodilatory shock. Your patient's normal kidney and liver function and lack of allergies don't change that limit.\n\nHere are some possible explanations:\n- **Typo or unit error.** The intended dose may have been 2.5, 5, or 10 mg. The usual start is 5 mg once daily, or 2.5 mg for frail patients or those with small body size. The usual titration goes up to 10 mg.\n- **Different drug.** Another agent may have been intended.\n- **Intentional high dose.** If your attending wants more than 10 mg, I'd suggest confirming that with them and with pharmacy before ordering. Adding a second antihypertensive class is usually preferred over going past the maximum.\n\nTell me the corrected dose, for example \"amlodipine 5 mg PO once daily\", and I'll enter it right away. If the 30 mg is intentional, I'd still want your attending's or pharmacy's confirmation first."
      }
